WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.002
-Hey, looking good, buddy.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.503
Welcome to the show.

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:05.905
Thank you so much
for doing this.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:09.174
I appreciate this. I like
the little Halloween back there.

00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:10.910
This is very --

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.079
See? Come on. You're a good man.

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:14.480
I want to get into
so many things.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.784
But first, the final
presidential debate was tonight.

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:21.220
And we shot this interview
earlier today before the debate.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.591
But you've been live-tweeting
every debate so far.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.192
It is fantastic.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.095
Is it therapeutic for you
in a way?

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.596
-It's weird, yes.

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:34.033
It's very therapeutic
in that I tweet stuff out about

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.135
what I'm seeing
to make a "joke" about it,

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:39.205
but then I'm realizing and if
you watch as the night goes on,

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.074
my tweets turn into,
"Did he just s--

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:44.044
like, that's insane
what he just said."

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.078
And then I'm looking at
the replies,

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.381
and everyone on Twitter
is going,

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.250
"Yeah, that was -- it's not you.
You're not hallucinating.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.919
He said that. It's okay."

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:55.721
Like, we now need Twitter

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:58.792
for reassurance
as to what reality is.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:02.161
-In this debate tonight, they
are introducing muted mikes,

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:03.329
the mute button.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:06.632
-I mean, there's a mute button
at a debate!

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:08.001
That's where we are!

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.469
-What is going on?

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:13.106
What would you do if -- What
measures would you put in place

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:14.674
to make sure the American people

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:16.542
got the debate
that they deserve?

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:19.279
-I would have
little flamethrowers

00:01:19.279 --> 00:01:21.714
facing up from the podium
that they could see.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.048
There's no surprise here.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:25.551
And first infraction,
we just light the pilot light.

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:27.019
You see the little
pilot light come on.

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:28.921
Second one, quick little burst.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:30.122
And then third one,

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:32.792
we just go full Sigourney Weaver
in "Alien."

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:35.862
-[ Laughs ]
-Just blast everything.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:38.131
-Little burst.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.132
-One of the things we always
end up talking about

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:41.867
is music on our show.

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:44.237
But you said
that you are impressed with

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:48.475
Biden's music choices
when compared to Trump's.

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:51.310
-I mean, not just Biden's
music choices

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.479
but Biden's music access.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:56.048
Did you see the ad
for the Blind Pig

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:58.751
where in the same ad --
-Yes.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.754
-Iggy Pop, The Breeders, the
Pixies, and the Beastie Boys!

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.523
-Beastie Boys!
-In the same ad.

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:09.662
Now, Trump at best has
Lee Greenwood, maybe Kid Rock

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.397
and then that free music

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.232
that you hear podcasts
use all the time.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:15.601
Like, the standard rock thing.

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.203
Whatever it is
you don't have to pay for.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.706
And then whenever he uses
any good song,

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.841
the artist immediately puts --
"I am not --

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.243
I don't want my stuff there.
I don't want to be associated."

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:27.847
So when you watch the Blind Pig
ad, A, for you and me,

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:31.117
for Gen X,
that was a bunker buster,

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.918
having all those bands
in the same ad.

00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:36.155
We're like, "Well, you own Gen X
now. You have us."

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.590
-I couldn't believe that.
-But also you've got to know

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.092
that if someone our age
who is a Trump supporter

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:41.394
is watching that ad,
they're like,

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:44.363
"Everyone that I ever loved
through the '80s and '90s

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:45.898
thinks I'm an idiot right now."

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:48.634
-Yeah, like, Neil Young is like,
"Stop, just don't even try."

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.370
-No. Exactly.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.504
-I want to talk to you about

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.372
what you're doing
for your new special.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:54.707
First of all, congrats.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.575
Your Netflix special
"I Love Everything"

00:02:56.575 --> 00:02:58.611
was nominated for an Emmy.
Congrats on that.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.012
I haven't had a chance
to congratulate you

00:03:00.012 --> 00:03:01.547
and say that was awesome.

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.349
But you were in great company.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:07.186
Mulaney, Hannah Gadsby, Seth.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.955
-Seth Meyers
and Dave Chappelle.

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:11.858
-Oh, Chappelle.
Yeah, of course.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.792
-Who I happily lost to.

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:15.727
-You and Chappelle though --
Is this story --

00:03:15.727 --> 00:03:17.130
I heard this is true.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.166
Did you and Dave Chappelle start
on the exact same night?

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.535
-I remember it
as the exact same night.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.970
He said it was the same week.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.872
We both came out
of Washington, D.C.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:30.509
It was July 18, 1988, Garvin's
Comedy Club, Tuesday night.

00:03:30.509 --> 00:03:33.112
He was 14 years old. I was 19.

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.481
He went up.
-14.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.718
-He took the stage like he had
been doing it for 30 years.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:41.620
It just -- it was the most
natural -- you just --

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.056
"Oh, this person is going to be
a gigantic star."

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:45.858
He absolutely killed.

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:49.429
And then he told me later on,
because I said I was hanging out

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:54.099
with him recently at all places,
at Eddie Murphy's house.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:56.602
We were hanging out,
and it was the weirdest --

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:59.038
That's a whole other story.
-I can't wait, yeah.

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.106
Save that one.
-We were talking about that.

00:04:01.106 --> 00:04:03.409
He goes,
"Yeah, but what you forget is,

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.911
I had that great first night.

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:08.447
And then I couldn't get a laugh
for like a year later,"

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:11.117
which really -- which has
happened to a lot of comedians.

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:14.453
David Cross and Brian Regan both
talk about how the first time

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.421
they went on stage,
they annihilated

00:04:16.421 --> 00:04:19.292
and then they could not
figure out how to make that

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:21.694
happen again for like a year,
and that really messes with you.

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.662
-It really does.
You're like, "What did I do?"

00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:24.864
What was the secret
of that night?

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.432
Was I just in a better mood?"

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.801
That's so interesting.
-Yeah, but luckily,

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.170
on my first night,
I didn't have the disadvantages

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.238
that Dave Chappelle had.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:34.239
I completely ate it.

00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:36.242
Had nowhere to go but up.

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:38.544
-[ Laughs ]
You set the bar low.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.546
-Yeah, not only did I eat it,
I had that thing --

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:41.914
I'm sure you've had
this happen too --

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:46.185
where the one "laugh" I got
was from another comedian.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:48.121
But he gave me
that kind of polite, like,

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.757
"Ah, I see how
you were trying to get --

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:54.393
I appreciate that you were
trying to get to a joke.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.929
You didn't get to a joke, but I
will acknowledge the effort."

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.464
You've heard that before,
haven't you?

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:01.233
-Oh, it's my favorite. The left
side of the room at The Improv.

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:03.436
That's where all the comics
would be, and if you do a thing,

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:05.304
you would hear
somebody go like, "Ah."

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.205
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:07.205 --> 00:05:09.842
Ugh! That's the whole, like --
-That's exactly right.

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:12.178
-"I acknowledge your effort,
but you failed."

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:15.181
-But you're doing actually
a live virtual special tomorrow.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:19.051
-I'm going to be doing
an actual sitting down --

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:22.154
I tried to work out a way to,
like, walk around with a mike.

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:24.123
But I haven't worked --
This is going to be me

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.692
sitting and talking intimately.
-Yep.

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:28.193
-And I was very nervous about
doing this

00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:30.563
because I'm just addicted to
I go on a stage.

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:34.333
But I've been starting
to watch online shows.

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:36.469
I watched Brian Posehn's
special.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:38.237
I watched Maria Bamford's
special.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:42.008
And there's a regular show
called Keep Your Distance...

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:43.810
-Oh, really?
-...that does an online show.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.378
And they're really, really good.

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:48.046
And also Mike Birbiglia has been
working out ways.

00:05:48.046 --> 00:05:50.182
-I've seen some of
Birbiglia's things, yeah.

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:53.386
-You know this. Comedy always
mutates and changes its form.

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:56.155
And right now
this is how it has to be.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:06:01.427
And I really love doing comedy,
so I don't want to not do it.

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:05.931
So you're going to get to tune
in and see the terror of me

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:08.601
trying to do this in a way
I've never done it before,

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:11.003
which I hope that in itself
will be entertaining.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:14.039
-It's going to be great. I
cannot wait to watch on Friday.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:15.441
Patton Oswalt, everybody.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.810
His new special,
"Patton Oswalt: Arm's Length,"

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.213
is streaming live tomorrow night
at 9:00 p.m.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:24.050
at onlocationlive.com.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.551
Thank you so much
for being here, buddy,

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:26.552
and best to the fam.

